          "code": "CALCULATE_EXPIRATION_RANGE",
          "id": "1769452263895",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769452263896_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Based on the following prompt: \" {{$context.$variables.UserQuery}} \", \n1. Calculate a expiration range and set:\nExpirationStartDate in format 'YYYY-MM-DD'.\nExpirationEndDate in format 'YYYY-MM-DD'.\n\nIf the user is searching expired lots without specify any date set ExpirationEndDate with today's date and ExpirationStartDate to be a year before today's date.\nif the user is requesting to search during a next period of time does not include today in the ExpirationStartDate.\nIf it is not possible to calculate ExpirationStartDate the value will be ''.\nIf it is not possible to calculate ExpirationEndDate the value will be ''.\n\n2. Get Lot Number. Normally, the lot number is after the word 'Lot' or 'Lot Number'.  Even if the word 'Lot' is part of an identifier, it does not mean this is the lot number if the identifier is after the word 'Item', it means this is the Item Number, not the Lot Number. Example: Item Lot-101.\nOutput Format:\n{\n  \"LotNumber\": \"<lot number>\",\n  \"ExpirationStartDate\": \"<date>\",\n  \"ExpirationEndDate\": <date>\n}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"ExpirationEndDate\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"ExpirationStartDate\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"LotNumber\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Set Organization"
